#### 6.1.6 Safety Provisions


6.1.6.1 To ensure the safety and well-being of all beach visitors, the BMC shall ensure the display of the following safety measures:

- a) Lifeguard stations and watchtowers at strategic locations for effective surveillance and prompt emergency response;
- b) Clearly mark the location(s) of first aid station(s) with at least one paramedical trained personnel;
- c) Display of information of locations of remote controlled water rescue devices;
- d) Develop activity specific guidelines for all permitted beach activities shall be displayed at prominent locations to promote geomorphological, ecological, and environmental sustainability. These guidelines shall clearly highlight the Do’s and Don’ts;
- e) Establish and display a pre-defined evacuation plan supported by trained personnel;
- f) Locations of CCTV and AI-enabled cameras to identify potential emergencies and automatically alert relevant services; and
- g) Demarcation of zones as per activity (for example bathing zone, buffer zone).

6.1.6.2 In addition, the BMC shall disseminate the following safety related information to all beach users:

- a) Lifeguard patrolling schedule and coverage area under surveillance;
- b) Warnings regarding marine biological threats such as stingrays, harmful algal blooms, jellyfish;
- c) Details of the official bathing season(s) and permissible timings for safe swimming;
- d) Advance notification (minimum 15 days) of any community functions, seasonal festivals, or similar events. The BMC shall prepare guidelines and all such events shall be held in accordance with these guidelines and prior approval; and
- e) Prohibition of any events on accessible beach areas without prior permission of the BMC. The BMC shall monitor implementation of event specific guidelines.

