### 6.2 Stakeholder Education and Awareness


#### 6.2.1 Education and Awareness Activities


6.2.1.1 The BMC shall organize environmental education and awareness programmes to promote the sustainable operations and maintenance of the beach. The minimum number of programmes to be conducted as per the classification of beaches during peak seasons, shall be as follows:

a) Class  I —  ≥ 5 programmes;


- b) Class II —  3 to 4 programmes; and
- c) Class III — 1 to 2 programmes.

6.2.1.2 These programmes shall engage the diverse group of stakeholders, including but not limited to:

- a) Individuals and visitor groups;
- b) Schools, colleges, and other educational institutions;
- c) Local authority and their representatives;
- d) Hotel manager and tour operators;
- e) Beach management officials/staff (manager, lifeguards);
- f) NGOs and local community groups; and
- g) Representatives of special user groups and marine/coastal protected areas.




6.2.1.3 The focus of these programmes shall be to raise awareness on the following:

- a) Avoiding the use of single use plastics;
- b) Participation in beach clean-up drives;
- c) Conservation of beach and marine ecosystems; and
- d) Promotion of sustainable practices such as waste reduction, segregation and safe disposal.

6.2.1.4 The awareness activities may be conducted in both passive and active forms, as appropriate. The passive activities may include exhibitions, films, presentations, pamphlets, and brochures. The active activities may involve guided tours, educational games, theatrical performances, and community beach cleaning campaigns. The BMC shall undertake awareness, education, and training activities as specified in Annex E.

