### 6.3  Water Quality Assessment and Management


#### 6.3.1 Bathing Water Quality


6.3.1.1 The BMC shall undertake the following measures to ensure the safe and hygienic bathing water quality:

- a) Identification and inclusion of critical or vulnerable sites for water quality monitoring;
- b) Define and document the number of sampling locations to ensure true representation for water quality assessment, in consultation with the local pollution control boards or public health department;
- c) Ensuring water sampling frequency of at least 10 samples per bathing season or a minimum two samples per 30 days at regular intervals, whichever is greater. The reporting metric shall follow 95th
percentile approach. The 95th percentile calculation method is given in Annex F;

- d) Collection of 100 ml water samples at a depth of approximately 30 cm below the water surface;
- e) Water sample shall be free from oil, odour, oil film visible on the surface of water, floatable such as tar residues, wood, plastic articles or bottles, containers, glass or any other debris;
- f) Collection of water samples shall be assigned only to trained personnel, and water shall be tested by the laboratories accredited at the national or international level for water testing;
- g) Implementation of collection, preservation, transportation, analysis and reporting procedures in line with the guidelines of the Central Pollution Control Board, State Pollution Control Board or any internationally accepted or peer reviewed methodology;
- h) Avoidance of storage of microbiological samples at 0 °C, as freezing can damage microbial cells and alter their viability, leading to inaccurate analysis results;
- j) Public display of the report of bathing water quality status on a regular basis;
- k) Industrial, wastewater, or sewage discharges near the beach shall not be



permitted that could compromise water quality;

- m) If water quality test results fail to comply with the specified requirements of bathing water, temporarily increase the sampling frequency to monitor the incident. In the case of short term pollution, additional samples shall be taken; and
- n) Immediate action shall be taken to restore safe bathing water quality, including temporarily removal of certification flags until compliance is re-established.
NOTE — Temperatures above 4 °C may promote the growth of certain microorganisms, leading to an overestimation of microbial populations.


