#### 6.6.5 First Aid Stations


6.6.5.1 The BMC shall:

- a) establish well equipped first aid stations to ensure timely medical assistance for visitors;
- b) ensure that trained personnel and lifeguards carry first aid kit at each station; and
- c) ensure the availability of an adequate number of trained and certified staff members and lifeguards trained in first aid and cardiopulmonary resuscitation/ automated external defibrillator (CPR/AED), by an institution recognized



or accredited by the government or the Healthcare Sector Skill Council.


6.6.5.2 Each first aid station shall be equipped with, but not limited to, the following:

- a) Adequate first aid supplies (for example, bandages, antiseptic wipes, gloves, disinfectant and plasters);
- b) Cold and hot water;
- c) First aid bed;
- d) Splints and slings in various sizes and a dry ice pack;
- e) An oxygen cylinder of minimum capacity of 9 kg, an oxygen mask and a pocket CPR mask;
- f) Immobilizing equipment such as trauma boards, immobilizing blocks, trauma/spine boards or a spider harness; and
- g) Specialized equipment such as a shark attack pack.

6.6.5.3 All first-aid stations and equipment locations shall be clearly signposted to ensure easy identification and accessibility by beach visitors.


#### 6.6.6 Emergency Alarm (Beach Alarm)

The BMC shall:

- a) install emergency alarm systems at key locations across the beach, including high- risk zones, lifeguard stations, and main entry points. These alarms shall be easily accessible and capable of alerting visitors and beach staff in case of emergencies. The BMC shall also develop and implement standard operating protocols for activating beach alarm systems and for communicating instructions to visitors during emergencies. This shall include coordination among lifeguards, beach officials, and local authorities to ensure a swift, safe and organized response;
- b) ensure that the beach manager conducts regular inspections and functional testing of alarm systems and maintains records of maintenance.
- c) ensure that all emergency alarm systems are with backup power; and
- d) conduct periodic awareness campaigns through digital platforms, on-site signage, and live demonstrations, to educate beach users on how to respond when an alarm is triggered.

